Functional Dyspepsia
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Postprandial fullness/early satiety, Epigastric burning pain, Normal upper GI endoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-022
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, severe burning pain in the upper central abdomen causing the patient to stop eating for the past 48 hours, resulting in significant weakness.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe epigastric pain preventing nutritional intake and causing systemic weakness. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out acute peptic ulcer perforation, symptomatic cholecystitis, or an atypical acute coronary syndrome, and to safely establish symptomatic control prior to routine general practice availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a severe, constant fire burning in the pit of my stomach, and I feel completely full after taking just two bites of toast."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 4-week history of bothersome postprandial fullness occurring after normal-sized meals. Over the past 48 hours, early satiety has prevented the completion of regular meals, leading to profound weakness. Now experiencing severe epigastric burning pain that is not predictably associated with meals. Associated with mild nausea, recurrent belching, and frequent upper abdominal bloating. Complete lack of symptom relief following defecation. Total absence of alarm features including dysphagia, melaena, or unintentional weight loss. Normal baseline appetite prior to this episode.
Functional Status: Confined to an armchair, unable to perform standard household tasks due to weakness and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Decreased oral intake risking dehydration if left unmanaged.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient appears fatigued and uncomfortable, repeatedly pressing a hand against the upper abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is flat. Deep palpation reveals mild to moderate tenderness localized strictly to the epigastrium. No guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness. Murphy's sign is negative. Bowel sounds are active and normal. Cardiopulmonary examination is entirely unremarkable, with normal heart sounds and no reproducible chest wall tenderness. The presentation aligns with postprandial fullness/early satiety and epigastric burning pain; a normal upper GI endoscopy is presumed as the defining feature ruling out organic disease.
Environmental Observations: Multiple over-the-counter antacid bottles are present but reportably ineffective.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking daily Ibuprofen for chronic knee pain, which has been ceased over the last 48 hours due to stomach pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Functional Dyspepsia - Status: Exacerbated (Epigastric pain syndrome predominant)
Differentials:

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lack of response to high-dose over-the-counter antacids and the absence of localized peritonism or melaena, though an endoscopy is ultimately required to definitively exclude mucosal ulceration.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain strictly localized to the epigastrium without radiation to the jaw or arm, absence of diaphoresis, and strong association with early satiety and belching.

Biliary Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The constant, burning nature of the pain rather than episodic, severe cramping, and a negative Murphy's sign.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe exacerbation of epigastric pain and profound early satiety, leading to an acute cessation of oral intake. Urgent out-of-hours evaluation was mandatory to safely exclude an acute cardiac event (atypical myocardial infarction) and surgical abdominal emergencies (perforated viscus, acute cholecystitis). Physical examination confirmed localized epigastric tenderness without peritonism. Given the negative alarm features, the immediate risk is low, allowing for the initiation of neuromodulator/prokinetic therapy or high-dose acid suppression to break the pain cycle and prevent further dehydration over the weekend, avoiding a hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Pantoprazole 40mg daily and Metoclopramide 10mg TDS to facilitate gastric emptying. Advised strict avoidance of all NSAIDs.
Positive Behavioral Change: Divide your daily food intake into six very small meals rather than three large ones. By physically placing less volume into the stomach at any one time, you prevent the excessive stretching of the stomach wall that is directly triggering the false nerve signals of extreme pain and early fullness.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to assess response to medication and coordinate a specialist referral for endoscopy if symptoms persist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient begins to vomit blood, passes black, tarry stools, or experiences sudden, tearing pain radiating to the back or chest, call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A camera test (endoscopy) is required to be absolutely certain there is no ulcer or inflammation in the stomach lining.
Clinical Course: The medications should rapidly reduce the burning sensation and help the stomach empty normally, allowing a gradual return to normal eating within 3 to 5 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The burning pain should start to improve with the new medication. If you experience shortness of breath, a sudden cold sweat, or pain that travels into your neck or left arm, you must go to the Emergency Department immediately to check your heart.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The nerves in your stomach lining have become hypersensitive, interpreting normal food digestion as severe pain and extreme stretching. The acid-blocking medication will remove any chemical irritation, while the anti-nausea medication will physically force the stomach to empty faster, reducing the pressure that is causing your early fullness.
Actionable Steps: Completely stop taking ibuprofen or any other anti-inflammatory pain killers. These drugs directly strip away the protective mucus layer in your stomach, exposing the already highly sensitive nerves to raw stomach acid and worsening your severe pain.

